so I've been playing a lot of legends of  Zelda breath of the wild since I got my  switch and when I say a lot  I mean it's damaging my relationships  and business I'm obsessed and that's led  me to ask myself what is it about breath  of the wild that makes it so compelling  why are so many people infatuated with  this game right now so how to think and  I believe the answer has something to do  with the reason that we're drawn to  action movies comics and even working  out as well I think that we can trace  what the new Zelda gets right all the  way back to the very drive that pushes  us towards the venture and challenge  it's the same human spirit that's  summarized by Joseph Campbell's hero  with a thousand faces sounds grandiose  for a computer game but let me explain  [Music]  so according to literary theorist Joseph  Campbell every single story in films  books comics legends myths and yes even  games is essentially a retelling of the  same tale that tale is referred to as  the hero's journey or the monomyth and  while different examples of fiction  might veer from the structure more than  others  most will still make the same critical  stops along the way these stages include  the ordinary world the call to adventure  refusal of the call meeting the mentor  crossing the threshold tests allies  enemies approached the inmost cave the  ordeal the resurrection and return with  the elixir so during the first stage the  ordinary world will find our hero in the  normal environment and get to know what  their life is like while things are just  okay at this point usually the hero will  experience some kind of pull or desire  for adventure often the hero feels  somewhat out of place like they don't  quite belong this is a call in itself  but often it will be compounded by a  catalyst of some sort the discovery of a  mysterious trinket new information about  themselves or their world or quite often  the death of a parent in superhero  stories the hero will often receive new  powers  at first the hair will be reluctant to  accept this challenge they'll turn down  the offer of adventure and need further  persuasion and encouragement  this is refusal of the call often the  hair will meet some kind of mentor maybe  an older man figure they'll give them  words of encouragement and perhaps some  kind of trinket or weapon that it can  take with them to help on their way  this is meeting the mentor and in some  cases it will happen later in the story  crossing the threshold is the point at  which the hero accepts the road ahead  and embraces the journey sometimes this  will literally involve crossing a  threshold into a world of adventure  other times it will be a less literal  decision to try something new or take on  a challenge  repeating we're not in Kansas anymore  tests allies and enemies describes the  initial challenges faced in this strange  new reality often this means meeting new  allies and facing initial challenges and  obstacles that aren't too hard to  overcome  but they give a taste of this new world  eventually the hill will make progress  on their journey into the strange new  world and they'll begin to uncover its  core this is often described as the  inmost cave the belly of the beast as it  were and the most dangerous and crucial  part of the challenge the ordeal is the  biggest challenge of the hero will face  and one that will often leave them  battered and defeated it's at this point  that we normally see the end of act to  the point where all seems lost for the  hero what did you say to the kid it  ain't about how hard you hit it's about  how hard you can get hit  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  the next stage is where the hero turns  things around sometimes by accepting a  higher cause or having a personal  epiphany often there will be a literal  transformation and my favorite example  of this of course is Goku's  transformation into a super saiyan of  course this theme is also seen very  commonly throughout religion and myths  with many messiahs literally returning  from the dead only stronger and more  formidable than before in other  instances the hero may receive a reward  of some sort such as an elixir a weapon  knowledge or love this may be the  MacGuffin that motivated the hero to  leave in the first place  or it might be something unexpected  finally the hero will journey home and  return with a newfound power and  confidence completing the coming-of-age  story they'll often face down an evil in  the home territory and may face a  parental figure in order to become their  own person and mature into an adult they  are now masses of both worlds of course  sometimes this elixir is loved in which  case they'll likely live happily ever  after  so these themes are universal because  they speak to our unconscious desires  and commonalities in particular they  speak to the aspects of our psychology  that are shared across all of humanity  as vestiges of our evolutionary history  it's no coincidence that Joseph Campbell  was influenced by the psychologist Jung  who suggested the existence of a shared  collective unconscious Jung too pointed  out the recurring themes across culture  history and works of art in the form of  archetypal characters so we respond to  this universal story because it is the  story that we all shared when Humanity  was in its infancy we would all have  been born into small supportive tribes  and then would have been forced to  venture out into the world to discover  pastures new and new resources would  have battled with monsters forged for  food and become stronger and more  formidable in the process and this is  still a story to a degree we are still  forced at some point to leave our home  and to make new friends to decide what  we want in life and to grow as people  and we all strive for actualization that  point at which we feel we found purpose  and peace for more my many years writing  about health fitness and neuroscience  what I've learned is that a human body  and mind crave this challenge and you  expect  our body is constantly changing and if  it's not taking on new challenges and  having new adventures then it's moving  backwards it's up to you if you want  your body and mind to grow or decay this  is why the brain releases a reward  hormones like dopamine and serotonin  when we successfully accomplish a  challenge and it's why it becomes more  plastic when we try to learn new  subjects this is why we're more like to  enter flow state when we're an entirely  new environment and when it comes to  learning the brain much prefers learning  through action and doing rather than  reading and theorizing we crave physical  challenge and our bodies yearn to climb  and jump that's why I find it so  offensive that when we climb something  in public we're told to get down  Drucker's keep on park chorong so you  see this venturing out into the wild  outdoors might not have been  particularly comfortable or safe but it  is what our bodies are designed to do  and we still crave that kind of  adventure mystery and survival this is  where we get our urge to go out and  explore the world now and to take on new  challenges we could stay in our comfort  zone but then we would not progress  forwards as individuals or as a race if  we did not all share that call to  adventure then which humanity be where  it is today or what we won't still be  living in caves we choose to go to the  moon not because it is easy but because  it is hard this is the best part of the  human spirit and it's what exists in all  of us it's why we will continue to do  great things and it's why we will become  overweight depressed and mentally weak  when we don't venture outside our  comfort zones this is why I think Zelda  is such a home run for Nintendo it  manages to capture that feeling of  challenge of having to think on your  feet and discover amazing new things  perfectly when you play breath of the  wild you are living out that hero's  journey whether you're questing through  thick woods trying to find food to eat  and taking shelter from the storm or  whether you're finding the master sword  and using it to return to areas and  battle enemies that you couldn't fight  before  and it perfectly evokes that sense of  awe and wonder when he discover an  amazing new environment and this is no  coincidence either  shigeru miyamoto says that his original  inspiration for The Legend of Zelda was  playing in the woods with his friends  and going on adventures as a child even  the setting and the name alludes to that  original feeling we would have got when  we had to survive in the wild cooking  things to eat foraging for food and  stalking our prey Zelda breath of the  wild when you realize that while being  chased by an enemy you can drop a  combustible material to use as a trap  that's precisely the kind of quick  thinking that our brains thrive on that  makes them grow and develop  Zelda works because it gives us  challenge discovery in adventure even if  it is just a simulation the brain needs  that adventure and challenge to be happy  if you're not finally going calling or  striving for your own discoveries then  you won't be fulfilled  so forget comfort and focus on making  your life a grand adventure that way  you'll be happier and healthier in the  meantime at least we have games and  movies like Zelda and others for a  little bit of escapism and this by the  way is what the bionaire is all about  it's about becoming faster stronger and  smarter  it's about constantly challenging the  body and mind the new ways to keep it  growing and by doing so I think that we  can all of life's that a little bit more  like the lives of our heroes whether  that means discovering new ideas through  coding and philosophy escaping the  streets below by climbing and jumping  across rooftops or escaping the 9 to 5  so we can work throughout the world  that's the other thing I like about the  Zelda games they all serve as a perfect  metaphor for training and how it relates  to freedom and possibilities all of the  experiences you have in Zelda  make you stronger just as they do in  real life and the stronger you become  the more environments you can tackle by  increasing your stamina you can climb  higher by improving your combat you can  bencher through land swarming with  enemies and by improving your resilience  you can Trek through deserts and  volcanoes I'm happiest when I'm in the  gym or when I'm learning a new  programming language because that's when  I'm training that's when I'm growing and  that's when life is less of a boring  daily grind and more akin to of being a  warrior or  when people ask me why I work out the  best reason I can give is that so if I  ever met Batman we'd have something in  common  I think life should be an adventure and  I see no reason why it can't be thanks a  ton for hearing me out guys hope you  found that interesting and maybe a  little bit inspiring if you did please  consider liking and sharing this video  maybe leave a comment down below in the  comment section and I'll try and get  back to you perhaps consider follow me  on Twitter and Facebook I post my  workouts and my daily work etc on there  so you can see that and yeah I've got  plenty more like this on the way more  training videos more philosophy  psychology neuroscience transhumanism  technology working out you name it so  stay tuned and I'll see you for the next  one thanks for much for watching and bye  for now
 

